{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are too low in the first ~10–15 days during upswings (+~1 to +3 €/t typical), but become too high beyond ~day 15–30 when the market turns down (often +~4 to +10 €/t by the end). Apply a bias that shifts from + (early) to − (late) depending on recent trend.",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts late to regime changes. After a local peak, it keeps projecting elevated/flat prices instead of the observed sharp drawdown. Add a ‘trend-break’ detector (e.g., if recent momentum weakens) and force earlier downward adjustment.",
    "Oversmoothing/underestimating volatility: true outcomes show abrupt drops; forecasts remain clustered around 70–73. Increase downside volatility allowance when recent range expands; widen bands and pull the mean lower when downside tail risk rises.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model mean-reverts toward a high level (~71–72) even when history starts rolling over. Anchor mean reversion to a shorter-term mean (e.g., 5–10d) when momentum is negative, rather than a fixed long-run level.",
    "Spike handling: after short-lived rallies/spikes, the model extrapolates the higher level too long. Use a spike-decay rule: if a spike occurs without follow-through (next 2–3 days not making new highs), decay forecasts back toward pre-spike levels quickly (within ~5–10 days)."
  ]
}
